en.body:
Candle dim, crickets cold, the curtain’s shadow thin;
the wine’s last haze still holds a little warmth.
The brush stops. The paper resists. The ink swallows first.
Still unsealed, I fold it up again;
with each fold, one more door.

It is not that the east wind broke it off first;
I fear the flower may leave its roots for me.
Do not speak lightly of new marks and an old key.
When we met, I was only ever a guest;
and a guest receives the kindness of the house.

Bright copper, teeth newly fashioned.
About to seal it, I stop;
still warm, it is flicked between my fingers.
Suddenly, a small shadow before the lamp;
the letter lightly sealed, its scent faint;
and in a moment the tears have come.
A spell of tenderness;
then the sense that these are warm words belonging elsewhere.
In vain I have cast a new key from the old mould;
the grooves, layer on layer, all follow the same lines.

The night wears late.
I wipe away the tears, trim the wick again.
What can be done: the night so long, the paper so short.
Copy out, cross out again;
I fear the deeper words may be misread;
plainer words stay safe.
Tomorrow, when the copper passes into the other’s hands,
it will be taken for a promise good for a lifetime.
After the lamp is out, all I urge is: eat well.

Once we sat together silent in the empty corridor;
back then I thought it only ordinary.
The lone lamp’s red tears joined in our counsel.
I did not know autumn was half gone;
I only felt the nights begin to lengthen.

Since parting, letters and news have vanished.
Meeting people, I am afraid to speak of the west window.
Now and then a broken dream carries me back upstream through the years.
What I would forget comes back with me to the pillow;
shunning every trace, I find myself paired with it still.

Once, leaning on a little wine, we went by moonlight;
shoulder to shoulder, leaning together as the watch ran late.
At the feast, when people asked, I did not conceal it.
On the snow and mud, who was first to leave?
From then on, each in our own wind and mist.

Elsewhere the stars have not changed;
down in the world, how many years have turned over.
The old tide still carries a face flushed with wine.
The wish to forget has become this lyric;
face to face, our temples are the first to grey.

In the old pot, the orchid transplanted;
more than once, spring’s trace went awry.
The neighbouring garden takes a new division;
a passer-by breaks off an old root.
At the empty window, an inch of earth remains;
behind the closed door, each to our own dawns and dusks.
After we parted, I moved to a new town;
years passed; nothing like the old garden.

With an empty purse I go farther still;
dust accumulates with every year.
The pot breaks, leaving not one shard;
the roots that remain have nothing left to hold.

Your letter arrived today;
spring mud still on my hands.
My little daughter copies from a fresh writing model;
the potted blossoms fall and thin.

The moon was bright in the empty corridor;
by the lamp we spoke in tenderness.
Orchids green before the window;
thick morning dew beyond the eaves.
The watch ran low; no one had left;
deep meaning gathered in the eyes.
Your letter has come a thousand li;
every word an autumn long ago.
I open the pages, my heart already far away;
I set down the brush; there is no answering in kind.
It is not that I have nothing to say;
wind passes; fallen blossoms rest.

I close the pages; dusk is coming.
My little daughter asks for the leftover verses.
In the old pot the flowers are over;
spring comes, and shoots grow again.
The watering is a thing of the past;
the plain heart, after all, has not changed.
Your boat heads for the open sea;
I too have a horizon of my own.

Then, a parting beneath the window;
now, rain before the eaves.
I fear to hear the old tune;
note after note hastens the dawn.
The lingering sound will not break off,
winding into threads that twist the heart.
A lone lamp, cold against the wall;
lighting the wall as though lighting you.

Where is the self I was, now?
Lone shadows, each at a loss.
I push the window open: mountains like a sea;
all the ages, one faint string.
This sound has still not ceased;
here in the world, no reckoning of years.
I turn an ear, unsure if something answers.
Where is the lingering sound carried?

By lamplight I read the old poems;
only deep in the night could I sleep.
Back then, none of it seemed any different;
what has passed I give over to the years.
I have moved somewhere new again;
you still put the old pages in order.
The child hurries me towards sleep;
the orchid stands before the window.
Do not reach for the willow before the eaves.
Do not count the days of falling petals.
Each crosses the river of forgetting alone;
each has a shore to come home to.

Shadow of the curtain, several nights of long talk;
the first dew wetting the rail.
The words not finished — the breathing settled first.
Deep in dream, knowing nothing;
the moon up, the night nearly gone.

Some other day the heavy curtain will fall to the floor,
fall exactly as it did before.
A hand beside the lamp, turning a few times.
Tomorrow the wind may blow hard;
for now, shut the window well.
